<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamasee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yamasee were a Native American people of the coastal region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. Allied with the English of Carolina, they raided Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and its Native peoples for captives in the late 17th and early 18th centuries. Abuses by English traders led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee-war">Yamasee War</a> of 1715–1717, after which most survivors moved to Spanish Florida.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Yamasee formed as a multiethnic confederation of survivors from several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, among them Ocute, Altamaha and Ichisi, in what has been called the Mississippian shatter zone. European expeditions, diseases and trade had weakened these societies, and slave raids by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westo">Westo</a>, an Iroquoian people who sold captives to the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, completed their collapse from about 1660. Spanish documents first named the Yamasis in 1663, when they lived in five villages along the Atlantic coast near the present border of Georgia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Yamasee joined other peoples at the Franciscan missions of Spanish Florida for protection, but they resisted conversion and sometimes refused the labour the missions demanded. After the English settled Carolina in 1670 and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westo">Westo</a> in 1674, the British offered an alternative and sold them guns. In 1687 some Spaniards tried to send Yamasee captives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>; the people revolted and moved into Carolina, founding villages such as Pocotaligo, Tolemato and Topiqui in Beaufort County. In the early 18th century they numbered more than 4,000.</p><p>The historian Alan Gallay estimated that between 1670 and 1715 some 24,000 to 51,000 captive Indians were exported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, and Yamasee raiders were among the largest suppliers, selling captives in Charles Town, many of whom were resold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>. Indebted to traders through duplicitous practices, the Yamasee built a coalition of many peoples and attacked in April 1715. Governor Charles Craven defeated them at Salkehatchie on the Combahee River and drove them across the Savannah River. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic and raids by John Palmer in 1727 scattered the Florida Yamasee, some joining the Seminole or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, and about ninety sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gave up St. Augustine in 1763.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Little of the Yamasee language survives, partly in the works of the missionary Domingo Báez, and scholars disagree whether it was Muskogean, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a>, or something else. The Yamasee Archeological Project, begun in 1989, located a dozen village sites, and Pocosabo and Altamaha are listed on the National Register of Historic Places. The name survives in the town of Yemassee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, and in William Gilmore Simms's novel The Yemassee of 1835. Their descendants are likely among the Seminole Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seminole-tribe-of-florida">Seminole Tribe of Florida</a> and the Miccosukee Tribe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamasee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1702 James Moore's army of Carolina colonists and <b>Yamasee</b>, Tallapoosa, Alabama and other Creek warriors razed the town but could not take the fort; in 1704 his raids into Apalachee Province destroyed most of the remaining missions and killed or enslaved most of their people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Epidemics and, after 1700, slave raids by Muscogee and <b>Yamasee</b> allies of English Carolina destroyed them.</blockquote><a href="/state/calusa">Calusa</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial forces with <b>Yamasee</b> allies destroyed their stronghold of Neoheroka in 1713.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
